Below is a self-contained Rust function that computes a time-based moving average over the "value" column of a Polars DataFrame using timestamps from a time column. It uses a deque to maintain the current window (so the window is computed by elapsed time, not by row count). The function supports timestamps stored as Date64/Datetime/Int64 (epoch ms) or UTF-8 ISO-8601 / RFC3339 strings. Nulls in the value column are ignored from the average (if all values in the window are null the result is null). The result is appended to the original DataFrame as a new Float64 column.

Notes:
- Requires the polars crate and chrono. Example Cargo.toml dependencies:
  - polars = { version = "0.30", features = ["dtype-datetime"] }  (pick a recent polars, 2023+)
  - chrono = "0.4"
- The window is passed as chrono::Duration.

Function:

```rust
use polars::prelude::*;
use chrono::{DateTime, Duration};
use std::collections::VecDeque;

/// Compute a time-based moving average of the `value_col` over a window of duration `window`.
///
/// - df: input DataFrame
/// - time_col: column name containing timestamps (Date64/Datetime/Int64 epoch ms or UTF-8 ISO-8601/RFC3339 strings)
/// - value_col: column name containing numeric values (Int*, Float*)
/// - window: chrono::Duration specifying the lookback window (e.g. Duration::minutes(5))
/// - out_col: output column name to add to returned DataFrame
///
/// Returns a new DataFrame with `out_col` appended (Float64, nullable).
///
/// Behavior:
/// - Rows are processed in increasing timestamp order (timestamps are sorted).
/// - For each row, the window includes rows with timestamp >= current_timestamp - window.
/// - Null timestamps produce a null result for that row.
/// - Null values do not contribute to the average; if no non-null values are in the window, result is null.
pub fn time_based_moving_average(
    df: &DataFrame,
    time_col: &str,
    value_col: &str,
    window: Duration,
    out_col: &str,
) -> PolarsResult<DataFrame> {
    // Extract columns
    let ts_series = df.column(time_col)?;
    let val_series = df.column(value_col)?;
    let n = df.height();

    // Helper to convert AnyValue timestamp to Option<i64> epoch millis
    let mut times_ms: Vec<Option<i64>> = Vec::with_capacity(n);
    for i in 0..n {
        match ts_series.get(i) {
            AnyValue::Null => times_ms.push(None),
            AnyValue::Date64(ms) => times_ms.push(Some(ms)),
            AnyValue::Int64(v) => times_ms.push(Some(v)),
            // Datetime variant stores epoch and timezone info; second field is Option<&str> in some versions
            AnyValue::Datetime(ms, _) => times_ms.push(Some(ms)),
            AnyValue::Utf8(s) => {
                // Try parsing RFC3339/ISO-8601 string
                match DateTime::parse_from_rfc3339(s) {
                    Ok(dt) => times_ms.push(Some(dt.timestamp_millis())),
                    Err(_) => {
                        // As a fallback, try parsing as naive local "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"
                        if let Ok(dt) = DateTime::parse_from_str(s, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %:z") {
                            times_ms.push(Some(dt.timestamp_millis()))
                        } else if let Ok(naive_dt) = chrono::NaiveDateTime::parse_from_str(s, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S") {
                            // assume UTC for naive timestamps
                            times_ms.push(Some(DateTime::<chrono::Utc>::from_utc(naive_dt, chrono::Utc).timestamp_millis()))
                        } else {
                            // Could not parse
                            return Err(PolarsError::Other(format!("Could not parse timestamp string in row {}: {}", i, s).into()));
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            other => {
                return Err(PolarsError::Other(
                    format!("Unsupported timestamp AnyValue variant at row {}: {:?}", i, other).into(),
                ));
            }
        }
    }

    // Extract values as Option<f64>
    let mut values: Vec<Option<f64>> = Vec::with_capacity(n);
    for i in 0..n {
        match val_series.get(i) {
            AnyValue::Null => values.push(None),
            AnyValue::Float64(v) => values.push(Some(v)),
            AnyValue::Float32(v) => values.push(Some(v as f64)),
            AnyValue::Int64(v) => values.push(Some(v as f64)),
            AnyValue::Int32(v) => values.push(Some(v as f64)),
            AnyValue::UInt64(v) => values.push(Some(v as f64)),
            AnyValue::UInt32(v) => values.push(Some(v as f64)),
            other => {
                return Err(PolarsError::Other(
                    format!("Unsupported numeric AnyValue variant at row {}: {:?}", i, other).into(),
                ));
            }
        }
    }

    // Collect rows that have a timestamp and their original index, then sort by timestamp
    let mut indexed: Vec<(i64, usize)> = Vec::with_capacity(n);
    for (i, t_opt) in times_ms.iter().enumerate() {
        if let Some(ms) = t_opt {
            indexed.push((*ms, i));
        }
    }
    indexed.sort_unstable_by_key(|&(ms, _)| ms);

    // Prepare result vector (aligned to original df order)
    let mut result: Vec<Option<f64>> = vec![None; n];

    // Sliding window: store (timestamp_ms, Option<f64>)
    let mut deque: VecDeque<(i64, Option<f64>)> = VecDeque::new();
    let mut running_sum: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut running_count: usize = 0;

    let window_ms = window.num_milliseconds();

    for (ts_ms, orig_idx) in indexed {
        // push current row
        let val_opt = values[orig_idx];
        deque.push_back((ts_ms, val_opt));
        if let Some(v) = val_opt {
            running_sum += v;
            running_count += 1;
        }

        // Evict old entries with timestamp < ts_ms - window_ms
        let threshold = ts_ms - window_ms;
        while let Some(&(front_ts, _)) = deque.front() {
            if front_ts < threshold {
                if let Some((_, front_val)) = deque.pop_front() {
                    if let Some(v) = front_val {
                        running_sum -= v;
                        running_count = running_count.saturating_sub(1);
                    }
                }
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }

        // Compute average
        let avg = if running_count == 0 {
            None
        } else {
            Some(running_sum / (running_count as f64))
        };
        result[orig_idx] = avg;
    }

    // Rows with null timestamps remain None in result (already defaulted)

    // Build a Polars Series from Vec<Option<f64>>
    let series = Series::new(out_col, result);

    // Append to DataFrame (returns a new DataFrame)
    let new_df = df.hstack(&[series])?;
    Ok(new_df)
}
```

Example usage:
- Suppose df0 is a DataFrame with a "str0" column of ISO-8601 timestamp strings and a "value" column of floats.
- Call:
  - let out = time_based_moving_average(&df0, "str0", "value", chrono::Duration::minutes(5), "value_ma_5m")?;

This will add a "value_ma_5m" column containing the time-based moving average over a 5-minute trailing window.

