Below is a self-contained Rust function that sorts a Vec<A> according to the order defined by a second slice B (which is already sorted). Items in A that do not appear in B are placed at the beginning, and the relative order of items in A is preserved (stable). The implementation is careful to remain efficient when B is very large: it only scans B until it has assigned an order to all distinct items from A that appear in B, so it does not necessarily walk all of B if A is much smaller.

This example uses the indexmap crate (version 2.x), a widely used crate whose v2.0.0 release was published in 2023. We use IndexSet from indexmap to collect distinct items from A; you could swap in a std::collections::HashSet if you prefer.

Cargo.toml (add this dependency):
- indexmap = "2"

Rust code:

```rust
use indexmap::set::IndexSet;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::Hash;

/// Sort `a` according to the order defined by `b`.
///
/// - Items in `a` that do not appear in `b` are placed at the beginning,
///   preserving their relative order from `a`.
/// - Items that appear in `b` are ordered according to the order of their
///   first appearance in `b`. If `a` contains duplicates, their relative
///   order is preserved within the group for that `b`-element.
///
/// Efficiency note:
/// - We only scan `b` until we've encountered all distinct elements of `a`
///   that appear in `b`. This keeps the scan of `b` small when `a` is much
///   smaller than `b`.
pub fn sort_by_order<T>(a: Vec<T>, b: &[T]) -> Vec<T>
where
    T: Eq + Hash + Clone,
{
    // 1. Collect unique items from `a`.
    let mut unique_a: IndexSet<T> = IndexSet::new();
    for item in &a {
        unique_a.insert(item.clone());
    }
    let unique_count = unique_a.len();

    // 2. Map each item (present in both `a` and `b`) to a compact rank
    //    determined by the order of appearance in `b`. We stop scanning `b`
    //    once we've assigned ranks to all unique items from `a` that occur in `b`.
    let mut map: HashMap<T, usize> = HashMap::with_capacity(unique_count);
    let mut next_rank: usize = 0;
    if unique_count > 0 {
        for item in b.iter() {
            // only assign a rank if this b-item is present in `a` and not yet mapped
            if unique_a.contains(item) && !map.contains_key(item) {
                map.insert(item.clone(), next_rank);
                next_rank += 1;
                if map.len() == unique_count {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 3. Create buckets for each mapped rank (compact: map.len() buckets).
    let mut buckets: Vec<Vec<T>> = vec![Vec::new(); map.len()];
    let mut front: Vec<T> = Vec::new();

    // 4. Distribute items from `a`: non-mapped items go to `front` (preserve order),
    //    mapped ones go into their corresponding bucket (preserve relative order).
    for item in a {
        if let Some(&rank) = map.get(&item) {
            buckets[rank].push(item);
        } else {
            front.push(item);
        }
    }

    // 5. Concatenate front + buckets in order.
    let mut result = front;
    for bucket in buckets {
        result.extend(bucket);
    }
    result
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::sort_by_order;

    #[test]
    fn basic_example() {
        let a = vec!["x", "b", "a", "y", "b", "c"];
        let b = vec!["a", "b", "c", "d"];
        let out = sort_by_order(a, &b);
        // Items not in B ("x", "y") come first (in original order), then items in B
        // in the order defined by B: all "a", then all "b", then all "c".
        assert_eq!(out, vec!["x", "y", "a", "b", "b", "c"]);
    }

