Below is a self-contained Rust function (with a short runnable example) that uses the Polars Rust crate to concatenate new columns onto a base table while ensuring a specified priority column order. The function preserves the relative order of all non-priority columns and places the priority columns in the exact sequence requested. If the priority columns exist in the merged table they will be moved into the requested block (inserted at the earliest original position of any priority column); if none of the priority columns are present, the merged table is returned unchanged.

Cargo.toml (needed dependencies)
- polars is actively maintained and had releases in/after 2023; pick a recent polars version in your Cargo.toml:
```toml
[dependencies]
polars = { version = "0.31", features = ["lazy", "temporal"] }
``` 

Rust code (complete example):

```rust
use polars::prelude::*;

/// Merge `base` and `additions` (by concatenating columns present in `additions` but not in `base`)
/// and reorder columns so that all priority column names in `priority` appear in the exact sequence
/// provided. All other columns retain their original relative order.
/// 
/// Behavior:
/// - Duplicate column names are not duplicated: base columns take precedence; additions only add columns
///   that don't already exist in base.
/// - If any priority column exists in the merged table, the entire priority sequence (only the ones
///   present) is inserted starting at the earliest original index of any of those priority columns.
/// - If none of the priority columns are present, the merged table's column order is preserved.
///
/// Inputs:
/// - base: base DataFrame (kept as starting order)
/// - additions: DataFrame whose columns will be appended if not present in base (preserving their order)
/// - priority: slice of column names (&str) specifying the exact order of priority columns in the result
///
/// Returns:
/// - PolarsResult<DataFrame> with the merged/reordered columns
pub fn concat_with_priority(
    base: &DataFrame,
    additions: &DataFrame,
    priority: &[&str],
) -> PolarsResult<DataFrame> {
    // 1) Build a merged set of Series: start with base's columns, then append additions' columns
    //    that are not present in base (keeping addition order).
    let mut merged_series: Vec<Series> = base.get_columns().iter().cloned().collect();
    for s in additions.get_columns() {
        if !merged_series.iter().any(|ss| ss.name() == s.name()) {
            merged_series.push(s.clone());
        }
    }

    let merged_df = DataFrame::new(merged_series)?;
    // list of merged column names in current order
    let merged_names: Vec<String> = merged_df.get_columns().iter().map(|s| s.name().to_string()).collect();

    // Find indices of priority columns that are present in merged_names
    let mut present_positions: Vec<usize> = priority.iter()
        .filter_map(|&&p| merged_names.iter().position(|n| n == p).or(None))
        .collect();

    // If no priority columns are present, return merged_df as-is
    if present_positions.is_empty() {
        return Ok(merged_df);
    }

    // Determine insertion index = earliest original position of any priority column
    let insert_idx = *present_positions.iter().min().unwrap_or(&merged_names.len());

    // Build list of non-priority column names preserving relative order
    let mut remaining: Vec<String> = merged_df.get_columns()
        .iter()
        .filter(|s| !priority.contains(&s.name().as_str()))
        .map(|s| s.name().to_string())
        .collect();

    // Build the priority list with only those priority names that exist in merged_df,
    // preserving the user-specified priority order.
    let present_priority: Vec<String> = priority.iter()
        .filter(|&&p| merged_names.contains(&p.to_string()))
        .map(|&p| p.to_string())
        .collect();

    // Clamp insertion position to [0, remaining.len()] and insert the priority block there.
    let pos = std::cmp::min(insert_idx, remaining.len());
    for (offset, name) in present_priority.iter().enumerate() {
        remaining.insert(pos + offset, name.clone());
    }

    // Reconstruct final DataFrame in the desired order
    let final_series: Vec<Series> = remaining.iter()
        .map(|name| merged_df.column(name).unwrap().clone())
        .collect();

    DataFrame::new(final_series)
}

fn main() -> PolarsResult<()> {
    // Example usage:

    // base DataFrame with columns a, b, c
    let a = Series::new("a", &[1, 2, 3]);
    let b = Series::new("b", &["x", "y", "z"]);
    let c = Series::new("c", &[10.0, 20.0, 30.0]);
    let base = DataFrame::new(vec![a, b, c])?;

    // additions with columns d, e, and an overlapping column b (will not duplicate)
    let d = Series::new("d", &[true, false, true]);
    let e = Series::new("e", &[0i64, 1, 2]);
    let b_new = Series::new("b", &["p", "q", "r"]); // should be ignored because base has "b"
    let additions = DataFrame::new(vec![d, b_new, e])?;

    // Priority order: we want columns ["e", "a", "d"] in that exact sequence
    // after merging, but all other non-priority columns must keep their relative order.
    let priority = vec!["e", "a", "d"];

    let merged = concat_with_priority(&base, &additions, &priority)?;
    println!("{:?}", merged.get_column_names()); // prints the final column order
    println!("{:#?}", merged);

    Ok(())
}
```

Notes and rationale:
- The function first constructs a merged DataFrame by appending only non-duplicate columns from additions (so base column values are preserved).
- It then relocates present priority columns into the exact order specified by the user, inserting that priority block at the earliest original index where any priority column appeared (so the relative order of the remaining columns stays the same).
- You can tweak insertion behavior if you instead prefer to always place the priority block at the front or back — change insertion index accordingly (0 for front, remaining.len() for back).

This approach uses Polars' DataFrame and Series types and only clones Series references (cheap, Arc-like), so it is efficient for typical-sized tables.

