Below is a self-contained Rust function that removes duplicate records by field `A`, keeping only the record with the highest `B` value for each `A`. It uses the polars crate (a modern data-frame library with active releases in 2023+). The approach is:

- build a DataFrame from the input records,
- sort by `B` descending so the highest `B` for each `A` comes first,
- take unique rows by `A` (keeping the first occurrence),
- convert the result back to Vec<Record>.

Code (with a small example):

```rust
// Cargo.toml (add this dependency):
// polars = "0.30"   # or any recent 2023+ polars version

use polars::prelude::*;

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
struct Record {
    a: String,
    b: i64,
}

/// Remove duplicates by `a`, keeping the record with the highest `b` per `a`.
/// Returns a Vec<Record> with one record per unique `a`.
///
/// Uses Polars DataFrame operations: sort by B desc, then unique_stable on A
/// to keep the first (highest-B) row for each A.
fn dedup_keep_max_b(records: Vec<Record>) -> PolarsResult<Vec<Record>> {
    if records.is_empty() {
        return Ok(Vec::new());
    }

    // Build Series for columns
    let a_vals: Vec<&str> = records.iter().map(|r| r.a.as_str()).collect();
    let b_vals: Vec<i64> = records.iter().map(|r| r.b).collect();

    let s_a = Series::new("A", a_vals);
    let s_b = Series::new("B", b_vals);

    let mut df = DataFrame::new(vec![s_a, s_b])?;

    // Sort by B descending (false => descending)
    df = df.sort("B", false)?;

    // Keep the first row per A (first = highest B because of sorting)
    let unique = df.unique_stable(Some(&["A"]))?;

    // Convert back into Vec<Record>
    let a_col = unique.column("A")?.utf8()?;
    let b_col = unique.column("B")?.i64()?;

    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(unique.height());
    for i in 0..unique.height() {
        // unwraps are safe here because types are known and we checked non-empty above
        let a_val = a_col.get(i).unwrap_or("");
        let b_val = b_col.get(i).unwrap_or(0);
        out.push(Record { a: a_val.to_string(), b: b_val });
    }

    Ok(out)
}

fn main() -> PolarsResult<()> {
    let input = vec![
        Record { a: "x".into(), b: 10 },
        Record { a: "y".into(), b: 5 },
        Record { a: "x".into(), b: 20 }, // duplicate A="x" with higher B
        Record { a: "z".into(), b: 7 },
        Record { a: "y".into(), b: 9 },  // duplicate A="y" with higher B
    ];

    let deduped = dedup_keep_max_b(input)?;
    // expected kept records: x->20, y->9, z->7 (order determined by sort + unique)
    for r in deduped {
        println!("{:?}", r);
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

Notes:
